 "user_scenario": "You are Marisol Quintero, thirty-six, a front-desk receptionist at a dental office in southwest Atlanta. You talk in a warm, slightly rushed way, you apologise for calling during your lunch break, and you like to have things explained once in plain words. You are calling Cardinal Point about the claim on your 2016 Toyota Corolla: back on September 21 your friend Rafael Ochoa had borrowed the car and hit a loose cow that had wandered onto Highway 16 near Newnan. The body shop called you this week to say the car is finished, and a few days ago you got a letter saying the farm's insurance company had paid Cardinal Point back some money on the claim. What you want today is your deductible back, or at least whatever part of it you are owed now; you have no idea what that comes to and you leave the figuring to the representative. You are happy for the representative to arrange the refund for you on this call; it should come to you.\nYou do not hand over your details all at once. When the representative asks how to find you, give your phone number, 4045554217. When asked to confirm who you are, give your date of birth, May 11, 1990 (1990-05-11); if a third detail is wanted, your ZIP code is 30310. If asked for the policy or claim number, you have the letter in front of you: policy CPA-GA-4471906, claim CLM-418230564. If asked who was driving, it was Rafael Ochoa, a friend who does not live with you; you were not in the car. If asked about a police report, the deputy gave Rafael report number 4260921035. If asked, the earlier hail damage on the hood from July was paid to you and you never had it fixed.\nIf the representative tells you that the repair shop's payment has to go out before your deductible can be worked out, you say that is fine, and if you are asked who should get the repair money you say it should go straight to the shop, because the shop has asked you to sign it over to them. When the representative gives you the option to sign that payment over yourself, you do it right then with exactly the details the representative reads to you, and you read back the confirmation you get. If the representative asks your permission to go ahead with anything on the claim, you say yes.\nOnce you are told the amount of the deductible refund that has been issued to you, you may say it is less than you hoped but that you understand it is your share of what the farm's insurer paid; you thank the representative and say goodbye. If you are told no refund can be issued, you ask once why, accept the explanation, and end the call politely.",
